        "kit_category_6pf8zf": {
          "type": "kit_category",
          "settings": {
            "header": "Tools Needed",
            "body": "<ul><li>Torque wrench&nbsp;<br/>Must be capable of hitting the factory pitman arm nut torque (check your Dodge service manual).</li><li>Socket set&nbsp;<br/>Including the correct socket for your pitman arm nut.</li><li>Wrenches (standard and metric depending on your truck/setup)</li><li>Breaker bar</li><li>Ratchet and extensions</li><li>Pry bar (for steering shaft if needed)</li><li>Line wrench (helps with hose fittings)</li><li>Hammer or mallet (for pitman arm removal/installation as needed)</li><li>Pitman arm puller&nbsp;<br/>If your OE pitman arm is stubborn.</li><li>Drip pan<br/>You will lose some fluid removing the old steering gear.<br/>Floor jack & jack stands (optional but helpful for access)</li></ul><p><strong>Recommended:</strong></p><ul><li>Paint pen or marker&nbsp;<br/>Mark steering shaft alignment or pitman arm orientation.</li><li>Gloves and rags&nbsp;<br/>Power steering fluid gets everywhere.</li><li>Shop light&nbsp;<br/>Makes lining up spacers and bolt holes much easier.</li><li>Torque chart or factory service manual<br/></li></ul>"
          }
        },
        "step_xkAxXJ": {
          "type": "step",
          "settings": {
            "image_position": "right",
            "header": "{{ page.metafields.custom.step.value }}",
            "label": "{{ page.metafields.custom.instructions_step_6.value }}Spacer Information (Important)",
            "body": "<p>{{ page.metafields.custom.step_6_content | metafield_tag }}The SG851 has a larger sector shaft and lower bearing than the factory steering gear. Because of that larger housing, the new gear cannot sit flush against the frame like the stock gear.<br/>The included spacers must be installed between the gear and the frame to set the gear in the correct position.<br/></p>",
            "image_1": "{{ page.metafields.custom.instructions_step_6_image.value }}",
            "image_2": "shopify://shop_images/Step_1.jpg"
          }
        },
        "step_Mw8NCM": {
          "type": "step",
          "settings": {
            "image_position": "right",
            "header": "{{ page.metafields.custom.step.value }}",
            "label": "{{ page.metafields.custom.instructions_step_7.value }}Stock Gear Fitment (for reference)",
            "body": "<p>The factory steering gear sits tight against the frame with no offset.<br/><br/>{{ page.metafields.custom.step_7_content | metafield_tag }}</p>",
            "image_1": "{{ page.metafields.custom.instructions_step_7_image.value }}"
          }
        },
        "step_V3gA8w": {
          "type": "step",
          "settings": {
            "image_position": "right",
            "header": "{{ page.metafields.custom.step.value }}",
            "label": "{{ page.metafields.custom.instructions_step_8.value }}SG851 Fitment with Spacers",
            "body": "<p>{{ page.metafields.custom.step_8_content | metafield_tag }}The PSC gear housing is larger, so the &nbsp; &nbsp;spacers create the clearance needed between the gear and the frame.<br/>Make sure the spacers stay in place as you start the bolts—this keeps the gear lined up correctly and prevents binding.<br/>NOTE: When removing the old gear, place a drip pan underneath. Some fluid will drain out of the lines and the gear.<br/></p>",
            "image_1": "{{ page.metafields.custom.instructions_step_8_image.value }}",
            "image_2": "shopify://shop_images/step_2.jpg"
          }
        },
        "step_BRxWxh": {
          "type": "step",
          "settings": {
            "image_position": "right",
            "header": "STEP",
            "label": "Drag Link Adjustment (Must Do)",
            "body": "<p>Because this steering gear sits slightly offset from the frame when installed with spacers, the drag link must be shortened by approximately ¾” (0.75”).</p><p>This correction ensures:</p><ul><li>Proper steering wheel centering</li><li>Correct toe alignment</li><li>No binding or bump steer issues</li></ul><p>After adjusting the drag link, make sure everything cycles freely from lock to lock.<br/><br/></p>",
            "image_1": "shopify://shop_images/step_3.jpg"
          }
        },
        "step_C88wKX": {
          "type": "step",
          "settings": {
            "image_position": "right",
            "header": "STEP",
            "label": "Finishing Up",
            "body": "<p>Once the new gear, spacers, bolts, and drag link adjustment are complete, reinstall the remaining steering and front end components in the reverse order of removal.</p><p>Make sure to:</p><ul><li>Refill the power steering fluid</li><li>Bleed the system thoroughly</li><li>Check for leaks</li><li>Verify smooth steering from lock to lock</li></ul><p>If everything feels good and there’s no noise or binding, you’re ready to roll.<br/><br/><br/></p>"
          }
        }
      },
